# Bandit Level 6 → Level 7

## Goal
The password for the next level is stored somewhere on the server and has all of the following properties:

owned by user bandit7
owned by group bandit6
33 bytes in size

## Commands used
- find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null
- cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password

## Explanation
'  find / ' (/) check the whole server
' -type f ' regular files only
' -user bandit7 ' user owned by bandit7
' -group bandit6 ' belongs to group bandit6
' -size 33c ' exactly 33 bytes size
' 2>/dev/null ' Hides errors such as "Permission denied"
' cat /var/lib/dpkg/info/bandit7.password ' display the content related to this path

## Result
When using ' find / -type f -user bandit7 -group bandit6 -size 33c 2>/dev/null ' commands that was required in order to find the path of the location of the password for the goal. And following up with the ' cat ' command above allowed me to find the password in question.

## What I learned
Finding content in the linux property that is owned by different users and group and using it as one command in order to get the password path.

## Problems and fixes
The sort of problem I had encounter was I was receiving denied permission and was thinking whether it was linked to ' sudo ' however through the command of ' 2>/dev/null ' it allowed me to by bypass that issue to retrieve the password code.
